{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This \"fixture\" is actually a bare-bones implementation with no version validation. The committed test requires that the version be applied in order, and that a persistence rejection does not commit state. So I need to rewrite the projector to meet the full spec.\n\nDesign:\n\n```js\nfunction copyState(state) { return state ? structuredClone(state) : null; }\n\nfunction validateEvent(event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  const { tradeId, version, type, payload } = event;\n  if (typeof tradeId !== \"string\" || tradeId.length === 0) throw new TypeError(...);\n  if (!Number.isInteger(version) || version < 1) throw new TypeError(...);\n  if (type !== \"CREATED\" && type !== \"AMENDED\" && type !== \"CANCELLED\") throw new TypeError(...);\n  if (payload === null || typeof payload !== \"object\") throw new TypeError(...);\n  return { tradeId, version, type, payload };\n}\n```\n\nWait — \"duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" That is, for an event where version <= current: return a copy of the current snapshot (or null if none? \"return the current snapshot\" — if there's no state… for example CREATED with version 1 when state exists at version 2 → return the snapshot). If there's no state at all and it's an AMENDED/CANCELLED → \"version gap or invalid initial event is rejected\" → reject.\n\nInvalid initial event: the first event must be a version 1 CREATED.\n\nCANCELLED is terminal: a later new version is rejected.\n\nPer-trade serialization: a per-tradeId queue of promises. Different trades are processed concurrently.\n\nCommit only after applyToStore succeeds. On rejection: don't modify state, and later events must still work. In the per-trade chain, if I attach a handler that always resolves the chain link, one rejection won't poison the chain for the same trade. In the old code, a single global `pending` chain was used and rejection would propagate… actually, since `pending = pending.then(...)` — when the inner throws, `pending` becomes a rejected promise. The next `.then` after a rejection skips that callback, so subsequent events are effectively blocked/lost (the returned promise resolves to undefined — actually, `pending.then(cb)` when pending is rejected: the new promise is also rejected… no wait, pending.then(cb) — the returned promise rejects with the same error, because the cb is skipped and there's no rejection handler. So all subsequent projects also get rejected with the old error → \"poisoned\").\n\nImplementation:\n\n```js\nconst chains = new Map();\n\nasync function project(event) {\n  const checked = validateEvent(event);\n  const chain = chains.get(checked.tradeId) ?? Promise.resolve();\n  const next = chain.then(() => processEvent(checked)).catch? \n```\n\nNote: we must not swallow per-trade rejections. The promise returned to the caller should reject, but the chain should continue. So:\n\n```js\nfunction project(event) {\n  const checked = validateEvent(event);\n  let chain = chains.get(checked.tradeId);\n  if (!chain) { chain = Promise.resolve(); chains.set(checked.tradeId, chain); }\n  const result = chain.then(() => processEvent(checked));\n  chains.set(checked.tradeId, result.then(() => undefined, () => undefined));\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nprocessEvent:\n\n```js\nasync function processEvent(event) {\n  const current = states.get(event.tradeId) ?? null;\n  if (current === null) {\n    if (event.type !== \"CREATED\" || event.version !== 1) {\n      throw new TypeError(...); // \"invalid first event\"\n    }\n  } else {\n    if (current.status === \"cancelled\") throw new Error(\"trade is cancelled (terminal)\");\n    if (event.version <= current.version) return copyState(current);\n    if (event.version !== current.version + 1) throw new Error/range \"version gap\";\n  }\n\n  const next = reduceEvent(current, event);\n  // validation of the resulting state\n  if (event.type === \"CREATED\") validate CREATED payload\n  if (event.type === \"AMENDED\") { ensure the resulting state's notional is a positive finite value, and owner is a non-empty string }\n  await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClone(event));\n  states.set(event.tradeId, next);\n  return copyState(next);\n}\n```\n\nCREATED validation: notional must be a positive finite number, owner must be a non-empty string. Invalid → reject (TypeError? \"Malformed events are rejected with TypeError\"). CREATED payload validation errors are probably TypeError. Version gap: reject — Error type not specified, so I'll use RangeError. Invalid initial event — TypeError or Error. Spec: \"malformed events are rejected with TypeError\" — this covers bad shape/type. For version gap/invalid initial I'll use Error/RangeError, and use TypeError for malformed shape. Hmm, to be safe: version gap → RangeError. Invalid initial event → Error. The committed test only checks rejection in the two cases shown.\n\nAMENDED: the payload may replace either notional or owner (or neither?). After applying, the state must remain valid: notional positive finite value, owner non-empty string. If the payload provides invalid values (e.g., notional: -5), the merge result is invalid → reject. Also, what if the AMENDED payload has unknown fields? reduceEvent currently spreads the payload into state — this could inject arbitrary fields. Better: pick only known fields:\n\n```js\nif (event.type === \"AMENDED\") {\n  const next = { ...previous, version: event.version };\n  if (event.payload.notional !== undefined) next.notional = event.payload.notional;\n  if (event.payload.owner !== undefined) next.owner = event.payload.owner;\n  ...validate...\n}\n```\n\nCANCELLED: should payload be empty? Not specified. Just ignore the payload, but keep the payload type check (object). Actually, should CANCELLED require payload to be an object? Malformed events reject with TypeError — I'll require payload to be an object (or null? keep it: for CANCELLED, if it exists it should be an object, or just accept it). Simpler: require payload to be an object (non-null) for all events. Hmm, the test's CANCELLED event might have an empty payload {}. Fine.\n\nActually, do I want to require payload at all? \"Malformed events are rejected with TypeError\" — I'll define: event is a non-null object, tradeId is a non-empty string, version is a positive integer, type is a supported string, and payload is a non-null object (or null if CANCELLED). Let's keep it simple: payload is optional. For CREATED and AMENDED, payload must be an object; for CANCELLED, payload is ignored.\n\nValidation errors for CREATED's notional/owner: reject with TypeError (since it's a malformed event). Good.\n\nAlso, validation should happen synchronously in project(), so that a malformed event rejects the returned promise (it becomes a rejected promise — fine). But should validation errors also enter the chain? For serialization of the same trade it's cleaner to validate synchronously before queuing, and return the rejection without touching the chain — a malformed event doesn't modify state, so order doesn't matter. Actually, even simpler: project() calls validateEvent synchronously; if it throws, return Promise.reject. That's fine.\n\nBut the semantic check of \"invalid first event\" (e.g., version 3 AMENDED on an unknown trade) — should that be done within the chain (since it depends on current state)? Yes.\n\nImmutability: input events — structuredClone when persisting. State is stored internally; all returns use structuredClone.\n\nEdge: `structuredClone` on the event — if the event contains functions etc., it'll throw. Fine.\n\nAlso `getSnapshot` returns a copy.\n\nNow, write student-tests.mjs with at least four meaningful tests:\n\n1. Per-trade concurrency: two trades make progress in parallel (without a global queue). Example: store callback awaits a deferred; send CREATED for trade A and CREATED for trade B; the first awaits the second's resolution — proving no global ordering. Example:\n\n```js\ntest(\"different trades progress concurrently\", async () => {\n  let releaseA;\n  const gateA = new Promise(r => (releaseA = r));\n  const done = [];\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async (tradeId) => {\n    if (tradeId === \"trade-a\") await gateA;\n    done.push(tradeId);\n  });\n  const pa = projector.project(created(\"trade-a\"));\n  await new Promise(r => setImmediate(r));\n  const pb = projector.project(created(\"trade-b\"));\n  await pb;\n  assert.deepEqual(done, [\"trade-b\"]); // b persisted while a blocked\n  releaseA();\n  await pa;\n  assert.deepEqual(done, [\"trade-b\", \"trade-a\"]);\n});\n```\n\nWait — b's processEvent is inside the chain: b's project is called after a has started. b's chain is separate, so b runs while a is awaiting the gate. But does b's project call happen in time? After `await new Promise(setImmediate)`, a's processEvent has started (in a microtask), and reached the store await at the gate. b's project is scheduled; its processEvent runs and completes synchronously (aside from a microtask). `await pb` → b is persisted. done = [\"trade-b\"]. Good, no sleep needed.\n\n2. Duplicates/stale versions return the snapshot without persisting:\n\n```js\ntest(\"duplicate and stale versions return snapshot without persisting\", ...)\n- project created v1 (store called once)\n- project a duplicate CREATED v1 again → resolves to the v1 state, no store call\n- project AMENDED v2 → state v2\n- project AMENDED v2 again (stale) → resolves to the v2 snapshot, no store call\n- count store calls === 2\n```\n\n3. Version gap rejects without modifying state; invalid first event rejects:\n\n```js\n- project AMENDED v1 on unknown trade → reject\n- project CREATED v2 on unknown trade → reject\n- create trade at v1; project AMENDED v3 → reject; snapshot is still v1\n```\n\n4. A rejected trade is terminal:\n\n```js\n- create v1, cancel v2, project AMENDED v3 → reject; snapshot is still cancelled v2\n```\n\n5. A transient store rejection does not poison work for the same trade or other trades (extending the committed test):\n\n```js\n- store rejects on trade A's v2 AMENDED; the snapshot is still v1; then AMENDED v2 succeeds; trade B is unaffected.\n```\n\n6. Immutability: modifying a returned snapshot doesn't affect the projector; the store receives a copy.\n\n7. CREATED payload validation: notional non-positive / NaN / Infinity, or empty owner → TypeError; state unchanged.\n\nThat gives 4+ tests.\n\nAlso AMENDED leaving a valid state: AMENDED with notional: -1 → reject; snapshot unchanged.\n\nNext, write the implementation.\n\n```js\nfunction copyState(state) {\n  return state === undefined ? null : structuredClone(state);\n}\n\nconst SUPPORTED_TYPES = new Set([\"CREATED\", \"AMENDED\", \"CANCELLED\"]);\n\nfunction validateEvent(event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(event)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  if (typeof event.tradeId !== \"string\" || event.tradeId.length === 0) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event.tradeId must be a non-empty string\");\n  }\n  if (!Number.isInteger(event.version) || event.version < 1) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event.version must be a positive integer\");\n  }\n  if (typeof event.type !== \"string\" || !SUPPORTED_TYPES.has(event.type)) {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${event.type}`);\n  }\n  if (event.type !== \"CANCELLED\") {\n    if (event.payload === null || typeof event.payload !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(event.payload)) {\n      throw new TypeError(`event.payload must be an object for ${event.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n  return event;\n}\n\nfunction isPositiveFinite(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0;\n}\n\nfunction assertValidTradeFields(notional, owner) {\n  if (!isPositiveFinite(notional)) throw new TypeError(\"notional must be a positive finite number\");\n  if (typeof owner !== \"string\" || owner.length === 0) throw new TypeError(\"owner must be a non-empty string\");\n}\n```\n\nreduceEvent:\n\n```js\nfunction reduceEvent(previous, event) {\n  if (event.type === \"CREATED\") {\n    assertValidTradeFields(event.payload.notional, event.payload.owner);\n    return { tradeId: event.tradeId, version: event.version, status: \"active\", notional: event.payload.notional, owner: event.payload.owner };\n  }\n  if (event.type === \"AMENDED\") {\n    const next = { ...previous, version: event.version };\n    if (event.payload.notional !== undefined) next.notional = event.payload.notional;\n    if (event.payload.owner !== undefined) next.owner = event.payload.owner;\n    assertValidTradeFields(next.notional, next.owner);\n    return next;\n  }\n  // CANCELLED\n  return { ...previous, version: event.version, status: \"cancelled\" };\n}\n```\n\nprocess:\n\n```js\nasync function processEvent(event) {\n  const current = states.get(event.tradeId);\n  if (current === undefined) {\n    if (event.type !== \"CREATED\" || event.version !== 1) {\n      throw new RangeError(`first accepted event for ${event.tradeId} must be CREATED at version 1`);\n    }\n  } else {\n    if (current.status === \"cancelled\") {\n      throw new Error(`trade ${event.tradeId} is cancelled and cannot accept further events`);\n    }\n    if (event.version <= current.version) {\n      return copyState(current);\n    }\n    if (event.version !== current.version + 1) {\n      throw new RangeError(`version gap ...`);\n    }\n  }\n  const next = reduceEvent(current, event);\n  await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClone(event));\n  states.set(event.tradeId, next);\n  return copyState(next);\n}\n```\n\nNote: for duplicate CREATED v1 with no existing state: current === undefined, event.type is CREATED, version 1 → re-persist. Hmm — \"the first accepted event is CREATED at version 1. Duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" A duplicate CREATED on an unknown trade isn't a duplicate (there's no state). It's the first event, so it gets persisted. Fine.\n\nproject:\n\n```js\nexport function createEventProjector(applyToStore) {\n  if (typeof applyToStore !== \"function\") throw new TypeError(\"applyToStore must be a function\");\n  const states = new Map();\n  const chains = new Map();\n\n  function project(event) {\n    let checked;\n    try {\n      checked = validateEvent(event);\n    } catch (error) {\n      return Promise.reject(error);\n    }\n    const chain = chains.get(checked.tradeId) ?? Promise.resolve();\n    const result = chain.then(() => processEvent(checked));\n    chains.set(checked.tradeId, result.then(() => undefined, () => undefined));\n    return result;\n  }\n\n  function getSnapshot(tradeId) {\n    return copyState(states.get(tradeId));\n  }\n\n  return { project, getSnapshot };\n}\n```\n\nAlso, should we validate getSnapshot's tradeId? \"getSnapshot(tradeId)\" — if not a string, states.get works anyway. If tradeId isn't a string, should it throw TypeError? For a non-string, return null, or require a string. Keep it loose: return a copy or null. Actually, for malformed input TypeError is safer: if (typeof tradeId !== \"string\") throw TypeError. That seems reasonable and not risky for the tests.\n\nKeep the old `reduceEvent` export? It wasn't exported. Fine.\n\nWrite the INCIDENT.md content.\n\nThen run the tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"I've reviewed the fixture. The committed test expects version tracking and clean rejection semantics, but the current implementation has **no version tracking at all** and uses a **single global promise chain** (`pending`), so when `applyToStore` rejects, the shared chain rejects, and all subsequent `project()` calls on *any* trade will inherit that rejection.\n\nLet's rewrite the projector:\n\n